/*
	RMSTEST 5 — CONTROL FOR TEST 2. Byte-for-byte identical to
	RMSTEST_2_circle0.rms except that the `circle_radius 0` line is DELETED.

	Test 2 showed that circle_radius 0 does not stack players at the map
	centre, which killed the reading docs/preview-design.md Sec.6.1 warned
	about. What it could not settle is which of the two survivors is right:

	  (a) 0 disables circle_radius and falls back to the DEFAULT unconstrained
	      ring — radius 40, variance 20 per guide:845/849. On a 120-wide map
	      that spans roughly 24-72 tiles from centre, which looks scattered
	      rather than circular. This is what Sec.6.1 asserts.
	  (b) 0 disables circular positioning ENTIRELY, so player lands position
	      like neutral lands do (random within the cross-shaped area,
	      guide:803). Also looks scattered. Sec.6.1 would be wrong.

	This map generates condition (a) directly, because omitting the attribute
	IS the default. So:

	READ IT LIKE THIS
	  Test 5 and Test 2 look like the same distribution
	      -> 0 falls back to the default ring. Sec.6.1 CONFIRMED.
	  Test 5 is visibly tighter / more obviously ring-shaped than Test 2
	      -> 0 does something else, and Sec.6.1's fallback is WRONG. Test 2's
	         wider spread would then be the cross-shaped-area behaviour.
	  Test 5 puts islands in the map's four extreme corners and Test 2 never
	  does (or vice versa)
	      -> that is the cross-shaped area showing itself, since guide:803 says
	         the cross excludes corners. Strongest possible signal, worth
	         looking for specifically.

	Nothing else is changed, deliberately — the comparison is only worth
	anything if the two maps differ in exactly one line.

	RUN ON: Tiny, 8 players. Please take the same number of seeds as Test 2
	(three) so the two sets are compared like for like.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
base_terrain WATER

create_player_lands
{
	terrain_type GRASS
	base_size 6
	number_of_tiles 150
}
